// EXPECT: FAIL
// ERROR_CONTAINS: ANUBIS_CONTRACT_UNPROVABLE
// SOUNDNESS: an EARLY `return` is discharged against the FROZEN call-entry precondition, so a parameter
// REASSIGNED before it launders a false postcondition. `g` reassigns `x` to -100 then returns it early;
// the entry `requires(x > 0)` still holds for the entry value, so `ensures(result > 0)` was WRONGLY
// proven — but `g(5)` returns -100 at runtime, and (contracts being compile-time only) nothing catches
// it. Worse, `h` composes on `g`'s false postcondition (`z = g(5)` assumed `> 0`). Now the early return
// over a reassigned parameter is rejected (ANUBIS_CONTRACT_UNPROVABLE) — return a local computed after
// the reassignment instead. (The tail return is sound; only the early return used the stale entry state.)
fn g(x: i64) -> i64
    requires(x > 0)
    ensures(result > 0)
{
    x = 0 - 100;
    if x < 0 { return x; }
    return 1;
}
fn h() -> i64
    ensures(result > 0)
{
    let z = g(5);
    return z;
}
fn main() { print(h()); }
